description:
FILED s543l (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-27), from the reviewing session's L2 (review of s543l, probes-1127/rl/p/l-03.pl row 3); base = tree.

`sysread` interrupted by a signal whose Perl handler RETURNS: perl's sysread returns undef with $! = EINTR and leaves the data in the pipe for the next read; PCL restarts the read and returns the data written later (the handler ran; the interruption is invisible).

REPRODUCER (perl 5.40.3 | ./runpcl):
    pipe(my $rd, my $wr); select((select($wr), $| = 1)[0]); my $got = 0; $SIG{USR1} = sub { $got++ };
    if (!fork) { select(undef, undef, undef, 0.3); kill USR1 => getppid(); select(undef, undef, undef, 0.5); print $wr "third\n"; exit 0 }
    my $n = sysread($rd, my $b, 100); print "n=", (defined $n ? $n : "undef"), " eintr=", ($!{EINTR} ? 1 : 0), " got=$got\n"; wait;
  perl: n=undef eintr=1 got=1      PCL: n=6 eintr=0 got=1
  readline under the same returning handler: perl and PCL both restart (perl's PerlIO retries a returning handler's EINTR for buffered reads) -- right.

DIRECTION (not built): %p-read-impl's sysread arm (the raw fd read) returns undef with errno EINTR when a Perl handler ran during the read and returned (the s543l rule-12 list: the die case already leaves the read), instead of SBCL's restarting read.  Measure what read() (buffered) does in perl first: it restarts like readline.

FREQUENCY: rare -- a loop around sysread retries on EINTR anyway (`next if $!{EINTR}`), and a program that counts on sysread returning EINTR from a returning handler is unusual; known from the review probe only.
